const semanticRows={
'section:ipsec-secure-media:0':['Separate IP-payload protection, header authentication and real-time media protection.','ESP confidentiality depends on the selected transform; integrity-only ESP is allowed, and anti-replay has integrity/state conditions. AH does not encrypt and authenticates selected IP-header material, which complicates NAT. SRTP media confidentiality likewise requires an encryption transform. A protocol name alone does not validate keys, peer authentication, replay settings or actual protection.'],
'section:ipsec-secure-media:1':['Distinguish transport/tunnel encapsulation and SRTP media scope.','Transport retains the original IP header while tunnel mode wraps the original packet. Typical gateway use is not a permanent-connection guarantee. SRTP is media-specific and does not secure signaling, identities or key exchange by itself; path-independent media cryptography does not guarantee every network handover succeeds. Both IPsec sections are marked optional beyond-textbook material by the profile.'],
'check:ipsec-secure-media':['Explain a broad ESP/tunnel-mode choice under gateway, NAT, confidentiality and authentication premises.','Qualified as a model-response reveal. Read with the stated requirements and preceding conditional teaching, the response calls for ESP with confidentiality/integrity and NAT traversal, not arbitrary ESP automatically providing both or tunnel mode alone solving NAT. The UI accepts no answer and grades nothing; this is not an implemented VPN, interoperability test or independent assessment.'],
'practice:ipsec-secure-media:practice-keyword-0':['Recognize the VPN term from the local protected-connection definition.','This clue repeats the expanded name and is deliberately elementary recognition. It does not demonstrate VPN configuration, confidentiality of every VPN technology, absence of bypasses or transfer to a novel scenario. Other options receive their own definitions after submission.'],
'practice:ipsec-secure-media:practice-keyword-1':['Recognize IPsec as the group of protocols for IP-layer protection.','The suite/protocol distinction makes the local label the intended strongest match; the response does not validate an implementation or prove knowledge of every IPsec service. Exact offered strings are graded with no synonym/free-text interface.'],
'practice:ipsec-secure-media:practice-keyword-2':['Recognize ESP as the usual IPsec choice when encryption and integrity are required.','Normally chosen is conditional instructional shorthand, not a claim every ESP SA enables encryption, integrity and replay protection. AH remains an alternative definition, not a separately keyed skill in these three questions. Scope labels and optional flags remain observations, not approved SY0-701 mapping.'],
'section:risk-resilience-governance:0':['Use qualitative likelihood/impact methods without treating ordinal scores as universal thresholds.','The approved organizational method supplies categories; numeric-looking ranks do not create precision. Insurance/contracts allocate specified consequences with exclusions and residual exposure; the text is introductory risk vocabulary, not a legal allocation, executed contract, insurance assessment or elimination of accountability.'],
'section:risk-resilience-governance:1':['Distinguish duplicate resources, continued operation through a component failure and reduced service downtime.','Redundancy is not by itself automatic failover or fault tolerance. Mirroring assumes the stated single-component failure rather than all shared-controller/common-mode failures. High availability is a design/recovery goal, not zero downtime, tested resilience or a demonstrated recovery target.'],
'section:risk-resilience-governance:2':['Understand the conventional SLE and ALE products and uncertainty behind their inputs.','The supplied fractional exposure and annual-frequency arithmetic is consistent. ARO 0.2 expresses an expected frequency, not a periodic five-year schedule; ALE does not predict the actual loss in each year. Asset/loss scope, coherent units, uncertainty and residual-risk assumptions matter. No real asset valuation, financial calibration, return-on-control calculation or calibrated future-loss forecast is approved.'],
'section:risk-resilience-governance:3':['Compare assurance against criteria, weakness discovery, authorized exploitation and objective-focused adversary emulation.','Audit sampling/independence constrain assurance; vulnerability findings do not prove exploitability. Penetration testing and red-team work require authorization, defined scope and stop conditions. These bounded comparisons grant no permission, establish no actual findings and do not make any method exhaustive or guaranteed effective.'],
'section:risk-resilience-governance:4':['Connect administrative oversight, investigation/care and business-context vulnerability prioritization.','Vacation coverage can expose hidden dependencies or fraud but proves neither. Investigation and reasonable action are related ongoing governance duties, not legal advice or mutually exclusive boxes. Priority uses exploitability/exposure, mission, reachability and controls; neither CVSS alone nor a universal multiplied score determines every decision.'],
'check:risk-resilience-governance':['Explain why active exploitation and exposure normally increase priority for equal base severity.','The model explicitly retains asset/mission-impact confirmation, so its answer is conditional rather than always-remediate-the-internet-host-first. This is a reveal-only discussion check with no scored response, operational decision, exploit validation or actual prioritization model.'],
'practice:risk-resilience-governance:practice-keyword-0':['Recognize a risk register as maintained risk/owner/treatment/status information.','A register is a managed record, not a single loss/annual-cost metric or a recovery target. Recognizing its name does not create ownership, accept risk or demonstrate governance effectiveness. This same-guide recognition supplies no independent question family.'],
'practice:risk-resilience-governance:practice-keyword-1':['Distinguish expected single-event loss terminology from annual aggregation and recovery targets.','The definition is introductory one-event cost recognition, not a numerical calculation. Source feedback also defines recovery alternatives after submission; those alternatives are related teaching, not evidence of RTO/RPO mastery or an approved teaching link.'],
'practice:risk-resilience-governance:practice-keyword-2':['Recognize annual expected-loss terminology without treating it as guaranteed yearly damage.','SLE and ALE use different time aggregation. The other offered definitions make the strongest local match clear, while uncertainty remains. RPO is a time-based data-loss target, not a cost estimate or restoration duration. No MTD/MTTR comparison or actual recovery result is delivered by these practice records.']};
